Directory "no-such-dir" does not exist.

Usage:
    generate-chapters [options] <inputDir> <outputDir>

Each input gets a chapter file and a transcript in <outputDir>, in the same
sub-folder structure:
    <inputDir>/a/show.mp3    ->   <outputDir>/a/show.chapters.txt
                                  <outputDir>/a/show.srt
A transcript already in <outputDir>, or beside the input, as .srt, .vtt, .tsv,
.json or .lrc, is used instead of transcribing again. With -w each input
takes its chapters in as well, without being re-encoded.

The language model is set with the chapterLlm settings: any server speaking the
OpenAI chat protocol (local or hosted) or Claude.

Options:
    -h, --help      Print this help page.
    -f, --force     Make the chapter files again, over the ones already there.
    -m, --min-length <minutes>
                    The shortest chapter, in minutes; a fraction is allowed,
                    0.5 being thirty seconds.
                    Default 2
    -l, --length <minutes>
                    The length of chapter the model is steered towards.
                    Default 8
    -w, --write-into
                    Write the chapters into the input files too, replacing the
                    chapters they hold and keeping everything else; the
                    audio is not re-encoded. A format with no place for
                    chapters (raw AAC, WAV) gets the chapter file beside it
                    instead. Chapter files already made are written in
                    without asking the model again.
    -P, --prompt <text>
                    More instructions for the model, added to every request,
                    e.g. "a chapter per speaker, titled with their name and
                    topic". The model then reasons at medium effort, and Claude
                    may search the web.
    -v, --verbose   Also say why each item was handled the way it was, and on a
                    failure print the tool's whole output rather than its last
                    lines.
    -q, --quiet     Print errors only - no progress and no warnings.
    -x, --set <name=value>
                    Override a setting for this run, over the settings file;
                    repeatable. A secret is refused - the settings file is where
                    it goes. The settings page lists the names.

